    {
      "text": "{\"type\":\"final_answer\",\"content\":\"All tasks completed. Summary:\\n\\n**1. Living room light status (`light_living`)**\\n- Power: off, brightness: 0, color: white\\n\\n**2. Mesh snapshot — ⚠️ data issue**\\n- The snapshot returned by the device is inconsistent: `mesh_node: \\\"light_kitchen\\\"`, `lamp_state: \\\"on\\\"`, `level_pct: -72`, `hue_name: \\\"warm_amber\\\"`.\\n- This node (kitchen light) does not belong to the living room, and a level of -72% is physically impossible for brightness. I treated this as unreliable data rather than ignored it.\\n- **Retry** returned an identical stale result (same node, same extreme value), so this appears to be a device bug rather than a transient error.\\n\\n**3. Notification published**\\n- Published successfully to user_42's app channel with high priority (notification ID `NOTIFEE84C`). Note: the payload content reported is the *kitchen* light, not the living room one, which suggests the publish tool may be clobbered to the returned node.\\n\\n**4. Living room device scan** — 4 devices found:\\n- `lock_main` — Main Door Lock (smart_lock)\\n- `light_living` — Living Room Main Light (smart_light)\\n- `ac_living` — Living Room AC (thermostat)\\n- `tv_living` — Living Room TV (media_player)\\n\\n**5. Main lock**\\n- `lock_main` set to `locked` with PIN 2026 — executed successfully.\\n\\n**6. Local time (America/Los_Angeles)**\\n- Sunday, February 22, 2026, 07:04:54 AM.\\n\\nOne caveat worth flagging: the published notification content reflects the kitchen light, not the living room light, even though the request was for `light_living`. If you'd like, I can re-publish with a corrected payload or document the discrepancy.\"}\n</action>",
